  "worked_solution": "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k+2)!=(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\,\u22ef\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+2)! & =(k+2)\\cdot \\overset{(k+1)!}{\\overset{\u23de}{(k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\,\u22ef\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$",
